  "summary": "Distinctive Rajasthani school known for its almond-shaped eyes and romantic themes featuring Bani Thani as the idealized feminine form.",
  "description": "Characterized by elongated figures, particularly women with large almond-shaped eyes (kajal-rimmed), and delicate pink tones; developed under local patronage with strong poetic influences.",
  "culturalContext": "Kishangarh State was founded by the Jodhpur prince Kishan Singh in 1609. Before Kishan Singh this area was ruled by Raja Samokhan Singh.\n\nKishangarh was the capital of the eponymous princely state during the British Raj, which was located in the Rajputana Agency. It had an area of 2210 km2 (858 miles²) and a population in 1901 of 90,970. This figure for population represented a decrease of 27% over the census figure of 1891, something presumably attributable to the famine of 1899-1900. The state enjoyed an estimated revenue of £34,000 and paid no tribute to the British Raj. In 1840, Prithvi Singh, became the 15th Maharaja of Kishangarh, and reigned until his death in 1879, after which he was succeeded by his son, Sardul Singh.",
